# ACCEPTED GAPS, each one a stated defect rather than a shrug. This case exists to hold
# the collision that produced every regression in this session — improved argument typing
# meeting a library-declared overload set — so its failures are the point of it.
#
# 1-2. AN INFERRED RETURN TYPE DOES NOT DISCRIMINATE A PRIMITIVE OVERLOAD.
# `tag(makeCount())` where `makeCount()` has no annotation: return inference gives the
# argument the type `number`, and the engine still answers `tag(boolean)` — declaration 0.
# arg_primitive derives only from a literal, a unary over a literal, or a declaration
# annotated with a bare primitive. An argument that is a CALL has none of those, so the
# primitive regime never fires and order decides. The fix is to derive arg_primitive from
# the argument's resolved expr_type as well.
consumer#viaInferredReturn() -> api#tag(number)
consumer#viaInferredReturn() -> api#tag(string)
#
# 3. A MAPPED TYPE DOES NOT DISCRIMINATE A NAMED-OBJECT OVERLOAD.
# `shape(r)` where `r: Required<Row>` answers `shape(Cell)`. The mapped type resolves to
# Row for MEMBER LOOKUP — that is what the mapped-type work bought — but the argument's
# type hash is still the alias, so it matches neither branch and both survive pruning.
consumer#viaRequired(Required) -> api#shape(Row)
#
# 4. A CONSTRAINED SIGNATURE ALWAYS BEATS ITS CONCRETE SIBLING.
# `widen(s)` where `s: string` answers `widen(T extends Row)` rather than `widen(string)`.
# This is the direct and known cost of the param_untestable guard in overload.dl: a
# type-variable parameter can no longer be REJECTED, which is what keeps the guard sound,
# and it therefore also can never lose — so it wins on declaration order. The corpus did
# not show this (remeda WRONG was flat at 558), and this fixture does, which is the whole
# reason to hand-build one. Removing the guard re-opens the 534-site regression; the real
# fix is an applicability test that can separate the two, not a change of tie-break.
consumer#viaConcreteSibling(string) -> api#widen(string)
